/*
The statement as provided is incomplete/ambiguous in English, but the intended task
matches the following standard formulation:

For each pair (a_i, b_i), define d_i = |a_i - b_i|.
In one operation, you may remove:
  1) one element i if d_i is in an allowed interval, or
  2) two different elements i, j if |d_i - d_j| is in the same allowed interval.

Allowed interval:
    x is allowed iff (x <= A) OR (B <= x <= 2*A)

We need the maximum number of operations.

Observation:
Each operation removes either 1 or 2 elements and always counts as exactly 1 operation.
So to maximize the number of operations, we should:
- remove as many singletons as possible first (always beneficial: 1 element -> 1 op),
- among the remaining elements, maximize the number of disjoint pairs whose difference is allowed.

Let "good" mean d_i itself is allowed. Then every good element should be taken as a singleton.
For the remaining "bad" values, we need maximum number of disjoint pairs (x, y), x<=y,
such that y-x is allowed.

This is a maximum matching problem on a line with interval constraints.
Because allowed differences are union of intervals [0,A] and [B,2A], and bad elements
exclude 0-difference being allowed only if 0<=A (always true), but duplicates would have
been good singletons already. Still, after removing all good singletons, remaining values
are just values not in allowed set; duplicates can pair if difference 0<=A, but then each
duplicate is also good and would already be removed singly. So remaining values are all bad.

For bad values sorted increasingly, the graph is convex/interval-like. We can solve with DP
using two pointers and greedy transition:
dp[i] = max operations using suffix starting at i in sorted bad array.
Transition:
- skip i: dp[i+1]
- pair i with some j>i where diff allowed: 1 + dp[j+1] plus whatever between? That is not valid directly.

Need interval DP? That would be too large.

But for interval bigraph on sorted line with condition depending only on difference and noncrossing
optimal matching exists. Then simple greedy for maximum cardinality matching works:
scan from left to right, pair earliest unmatched element with earliest possible partner.
This is optimal for one interval [L,R]. For union of two intervals, we can still process with
a multiset of waiting left endpoints while sweeping right endpoints:
for each value y, match it with the unmatched x having largest value among those with
allowed y-x, preferring the tightest feasible x. This standard greedy maximizes cardinality.

Allowed x for current y:
  x in [y-A, y]  OR  x in [y-2A, y-B]

We maintain unmatched previous bad values in a TreeMap(value -> count).
For each y in sorted order, try to find the largest x in [y-A, y]; if exists, match.
Else try largest x in [y-2A, y-B]; if exists, match.
Else insert y as unmatched.

Why prefer first interval [0,A]? Because larger x is always best; checking the nearer interval first
is equivalent to taking overall largest feasible x among union, which lies in [y-A,y] if any exists,
otherwise in [y-2A,y-B]. So just take greatest feasible x in union.

Complexities O(N log N).

*/
public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    static boolean allowed(long x, long A, long B) {
        return x <= A || (B <= x && x <= 2L * A);
    }

    static void add(TreeMap<Long, Integer> map, long x) {
        map.put(x, map.getOrDefault(x, 0) + 1);
    }

    static void removeOne(TreeMap<Long, Integer> map, long x) {
        int c = map.get(x);
        if (c == 1) map.remove(x);
        else map.put(x, c - 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);

        int N = fs.nextInt();
        long A = fs.nextLong();
        long B = fs.nextLong();

        ArrayList<Long> bad = new ArrayList<>();
        long ans = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            long a = fs.nextLong();
            long b = fs.nextLong();
            long d = Math.abs(a - b);
            if (allowed(d, A, B)) ans++;   // remove singly
            else bad.add(d);
        }

        Collections.sort(bad);

        TreeMap<Long, Integer> waiting = new TreeMap<>();

        for (long y : bad) {
            Long x = waiting.floorKey(y); // candidate in [y-A, y]
            if (x != null && x >= y - A) {
                removeOne(waiting, x);
                ans++;
                continue;
            }

            x = waiting.floorKey(y - B); // candidate in [y-2A, y-B]
            if (x != null && x >= y - 2L * A) {
                removeOne(waiting, x);
                ans++;
            } else {
                add(waiting, y);
            }
        }

        System.out.println(ans);
    }
}
